{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Java", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 8, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 3, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.2", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about Java.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Explain briefly how different programming languages work.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level. Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations. ", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob08re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "recap ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a synonym for summarize", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Let me try to recap everything so you can take notes!", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob08aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "allude ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to make reference to something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A code is something we use to allude to the statements that we write in a programming language.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob08aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "bewildered ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to be confused or disoriented about something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I heard Laura was so bewildered by it that I'm pretty sure it must be very difficult!", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob08aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "isolate ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to set apart or separate", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: With this implementation, developers succeeded in isolating the dependent issues.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob08aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "likewise ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "in addition", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adverb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Likewise, we see it as a generic program and its platform supports concurrent programming.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob08aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "manage to do something", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to achieve something despite the difficulties", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Java works with a platform-independence since it manages to reorganize the compile-link-execute sequence. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n4le03ob08aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Investigate about Java virtual machine. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write down your findings about Java virtual machine. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Create a poster showing your finding in a creative way. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share your findings about Java virtual machine with your teacher and classmates.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I investigate about Java virtual machine. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write information about Java virtual machine. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I create a poster showing my findings. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about Java virtual machine.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Modal verbs for probability: might, could, may must and can't", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Use the modal verbs to express certainty, probability and possibility about an event. In this case, you are going to learn 5 modal verbs to express probability. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Depending on the degree of certainty we have regarding an event, we can use <b>might</b>, <b>could</b>, <b>may</b>, <b>must</b> and <b>can't</b> to make a guess about how probable it is to happen. </br> </br> Remember the following structure when you are writing a sentence with a modal verb: </br> </br> <div class=\"center_text\">Subject + <b>modal verb</b>  + <b>verb in infinitive form</b> + complement. </div> </br> <b>Might</b>, <b>may</b> and <b>could</b> express the same as <b>maybe</b>. </br> </br> Example: </br> <i>Sara <b>might be</b> at Laura's house. (Maybe she is there.) </i></br> <i>Laura <b>may want</b> to know more about Java. (Maybe she is interested in knowing more information about it.) </i></br> <i>Juan <b>could study</b> with you today. (Maybe he has the time.) </i></br> </br> Use <b>must</b> when you are sure about the event you are guessing about. </br> </br> Example: </br> <i>Laura <b>must be</b> at Sara's home by now. She left an hour ago!  (The person is sure about it and the time is a good reason to support it.) </i></br> </br> Use <b>can't</b> when you want to express that something is not possible. </br> </br> Example: </br> <i>Laura <b>can't be</b> at home right now. She said she had to be there at 3 o'clock! (The person knows it isn't possible that Laura is still at home.) </i>", "type": "text"}, {"content": " You may be confused if you come to class tomorrow without things clear.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I'm pretty sure it must be very difficult.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Common content words and expressions used in Java", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "To understand what Java is and how it works you should have in mind some technical concepts. Here you have some of them: ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<strong>Class-based programming:</strong> It is also known as object-oriented programming (OOP). An OOP is a software design method based on the concept of <strong><i>objects</i></strong>. These objects have certain characteristics: the state, which refers to what the objects have; behavior, which stands for what they do; and identity, which is that special characteristic that makes objects unique. </br></br>  <strong>Concurrent programming:</strong>  It is a form of modular programming which doesn't require multiprocessors. As a server, it executes several streams of operations concurrently in overlapping periods of time to achieve a common goal.  </br></br>  <strong>Structured programming:</strong>  It is also a form of modular programming which aims to execute logical structures so that the program could be more efficient and easy to modify. You can use it in any language to avoid pitfalls of unstructured ones.  </br></br>  <strong>Generic programming:</strong> To develop efficient software libraries, they use this programming paradigm. It makes programs more general so that they can be more adaptable. As its algorithms are generic, they can be combined in several ways to produce a great variety of useful software. ", "type": "text"}]}], "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can explain what java is. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can describe how java works. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can identify and understan